Phase 9 proof run — preflight.sql and verify.sql exercised in BOTH directions
=============================================================================

WHEN      2026-09-16, this session.
WHERE     A throwaway loopback PostgreSQL cluster, 127.0.0.1:55439, database `phase9proof`.
          Created for this run, stopped at the end of it. No production credential was present
          in the environment (DATABASE_URL, DIRECT_URL and PROD_READONLY_URL all absent —
          checked before the cluster was started), so this was a development session, not a
          deploy session.

VERSION   PostgreSQL 16.13 (Ubuntu 16.13-0ubuntu0.24.04.1).
          *** DEGRADED. *** Production runs 17.6 and the phase prompt asks for 17.6. Only the
          PG16 server binaries are installed in this environment and the Docker daemon is not
          running, so 17.6 was unreachable. 16.13 is the same version phase 8's proof harness
          ran on (see prisma/drift-baseline.json, 2026-09-15). The objects exercised here —
          a btree unique index, a partial btree index, and an UPDATE — behave identically
          across 16 and 17, but that is a claim about Postgres, not something this run proved.
          CI's `migrations` job asserts its own major version and remains the authority.

NOTHING WAS RUN AGAINST PRODUCTION. The migration is AUTHORED, NOT APPLIED. Applying it is the
owner's, per run, under CLAUDE.md's production-database protocol.


THE FOUR RUNS
-------------

Chain built with `prisma migrate deploy` against the empty database: all 118 migration
directories applied, ending at 20261201000000_phase9_pickup_release_token.

PRE-STATE constructed to match the production pre-state the owner verified at 20:45 UTC:
  - the 20261201 ledger row deleted          -> 117 distinct applied names (production: 117)
  - pickups_token_hash_key dropped
  - pickups_live_release_token_idx dropped
  - ONE pickup seeded carrying plaintext in BOTH qr_code_data and qr_code_image
    (FK triggers disabled for the insert; a throwaway database, never production)

The seeded row is the point. `pickups` holds zero rows in production, and an assertion that
"no plaintext survives" is trivially true on an empty table — it passes while checking nothing,
which is the defect class this whole directory exists for. The row makes the assertion falsifiable.

  RUN 1  preflight.sql  on the PRE-state   -> 17 rows, ALL CHECKED, zero BLOCK.
                                              Terminal row: "17 assertions: 13 block-capable,
                                              3 reported" — matches the 17 rows emitted.
                                              Assertion 8 read "117 distinct / 117 rows /
                                              0 rolled back": the owner's pre-state, asserted
                                              rather than taken.
                                              Assertion 9 read "1 of 1 pickups" carrying
                                              plaintext — the scale of the irreversible UPDATE,
                                              known before it ran.

  RUN 2  verify.sql     on the PRE-state   -> 8 MISSING, 7 PRESENT, 1 terminal.
                                              MISSING: both indexes; the VALID+UNIQUE check;
                                              the partial index's VALID, predicate and key
                                              checks; "no plaintext release credential
                                              survives"; and both ledger rows.
                                              This is the run that proves verify.sql CAN fail.
                                              A verify that has only ever been run after a
                                              successful deploy has never been tested.

  -- `prisma migrate deploy` applied 20261201000000 --

  RUN 3  preflight.sql  on the POST-state  -> 4 BLOCK rows, correctly:
                                              both indexes ALREADY PRESENT;
                                              ledger ALREADY APPLIED;
                                              chain moved to 118 distinct, no longer the
                                              verified pre-state.
                                              This is the run that proves preflight.sql CAN
                                              stop a run.

  RUN 4  verify.sql     on the POST-state  -> 0 MISSING, 15 PRESENT, 1 terminal = 16 rows.
                                              Terminal row: "16 assertions: 12 physical,
                                              3 ledger." — matches the 16 rows emitted.

BACKFILL EVIDENCE (the reason run 2 seeded a row):

  before:  pf-1 | qr_code_data={"type":"autolenis_pickup","nonce":"k3j4h"} | qr_code_image=data:image/png;base64,AAAA
  after:   pf-1 | qr_code_data=NULL                                        | qr_code_image=NULL

  The row still exists; both credential columns are NULL. The UPDATE cleared real data rather
  than matching zero rows.

INDEXES AS ACTUALLY BUILT (read back from pg_indexes, not from the migration text):

  CREATE UNIQUE INDEX pickups_token_hash_key ON public.pickups USING btree (token_hash)
  CREATE INDEX pickups_live_release_token_idx ON public.pickups USING btree (token_expires_at)
    WHERE ((token_hash IS NOT NULL) AND (token_consumed_at IS NULL) AND (token_revoked_at IS NULL))

  The partial predicate is the resolver's WHERE clause in release-token.service.ts, which is
  what verify.sql assertion 5 checks clause by clause.


THE DRIFT GUARD — prisma/__tests__/phase9-proof-sql.test.ts
------------------------------------------------------------

10 tests, all passing. Exercised in both directions by temporary mutation, each reverted and
the restore confirmed clean (`git diff` = 0 lines):

  MUTATION                                              RESULT
  drop the migration from verify.sql's expected list -> 2 failures: "verify.sql does not name
                                                        20261201000000_..." and the phantom-name
                                                        check on the placeholder that replaced it
  shorten the prefix to 202612010\d{2} (phase 8's     -> 3 failures, caught by the anti-vacuity
    exact bug: two digits short of the 14-digit stamp)  assertion: "expected at least 1 phase 9
                                                        migration, found 0"
  make verify.sql's terminal row claim 11 physical    -> 1 failure: "terminal row claims 16
                                                        assertions (11 physical, 3 ledger) but
                                                        the file emits 16 rows (12 physical,
                                                        3 ledger, 1 terminal)"
  all reverted                                        -> 10 pass / 0 fail, git diff clean

ONE GUARD BUG FOUND AND FIXED BY THIS EXERCISE. The first draft refused the phrase
`/(one|two|three|four|five)\s+applied\s+rows?/` and fired on three CORRECT lines in verify.sql:
"one applied row PER phase 9 migration" is the per-migration idiom — it stays true at one
migration and at five, and the total beside it is derived from the CTE. Phase 8's guard
deliberately matched only the plural. The guard was wrong, not the file; the pattern now refuses
a plural total ("two applied rows") and permits the singular per-migration phrasing.
